// Basic's sorting and searching: a merge sort and a binary search over
// elements of any type. Rig has no generics yet, so both take the elements
// as a pointer to the first, a count and each element's size in bytes, as
// C's qsort and bsearch do, and a procedure value that compares two
// elements through pointers to them: less_int and its neighbours here, or
// one a program writes for its own type.
//
// The sort is a merge sort because it is stable: elements that compare
// equal keep the order they had, so a list sorted by name and then by town
// is by town, and by name within each town. It also takes n log n
// comparisons at most whatever the order the elements come in (n when they
// come sorted), where a quicksort can take n^2 on an unlucky one. What it
// costs is scratch memory, half the elements' bytes, from the allocator it
// is given, the context's unless another is; when that allocator has
// none, it sorts by insertion in place instead, slower but stable all the
// same, so sorting never fails.
//
// Elements are moved and swapped a byte at a time, since only their size
// is known. A comparison procedure may be given a pointer to an element's
// copy in the scratch memory rather than to the element in place, so it
// must compare what the pointers point at, never the addresses.
#load "allocators.rig";
#load "memory.rig";
#load "string.rig";

// The most elements sorted by insertion rather than by merging. For a
// handful of elements, moving each down past the larger ones before it
// takes fewer steps than splitting, copying out and merging, and asks for
// no memory. So the merge sort sorts runs this short by insertion, and
// sort asks for no scratch memory to sort this few.
SORT_INSERTION_MAX :: 4;

// Sorts the count elements of element_size bytes each that start at data,
// in place, by insertion: each element in turn is swapped down, a byte at
// a time, past the ones before it that it is less than, so it ends up
// after every one it isn't less than, and equal elements keep their order.
// It takes n^2 / 2 comparisons and swaps at most, so it suits only a few
// elements, but it needs no memory. Nothing is compared or touched for a
// count of 1 or less, or an element_size of 0 or less, so data may be null
// then.
sort_by_insertion :: (data: *void, count: int, element_size: int, less: (a: *void, b: *void) -> bool) {
    if count <= 1 || element_size <= 0 { return; }
    bytes := cast(*u8) data;
    for i: 1..count - 1 {
        j := i;
        while j > 0 {
            right := bytes + j * element_size;
            left := right - element_size;
            if !less(right, left) { break; }
            for k: 0..element_size - 1 {
                byte := left[k];
                left[k] = right[k];
                right[k] = byte;
            }
            j -= 1;
        }
    }
}

// Sorts the count elements of element_size bytes each that start at data,
// in place, by merging, using scratch, which holds count / 2 elements at
// least: what sort does once it has the scratch. Runs of up to
// SORT_INSERTION_MAX elements are sorted by insertion. Nothing is compared
// or touched for a count of 1 or less, or an element_size of 0 or less, so
// data and scratch may be null then.
//
// The two halves are sorted first, each with the same scratch, which is
// free again once a half is sorted. When the first element of the right
// half isn't less than the last of the left one, the halves are in order
// already, and there is nothing to merge: so elements that come sorted take
// n comparisons rather than n log n. Otherwise the left half is copied out
// to scratch, and the two are merged back into data from its start. The
// writing never overtakes the reading of the right half: after k elements
// are written, some i from the left half and k - i from the right, the
// next to be read from the right is at half + k - i, above k, since i is
// less than half while the left half lasts. When the right half runs
// out first, what is left of the left half goes at the end; when the left
// one does, what is left of the right is in its place already.
//
// An element is taken from the right only when it is less than the left's,
// so of two equal elements the left one, the earlier, comes first: that is
// what keeps the sort stable.
sort_by_merging :: (data: *void, scratch: *void, count: int, element_size: int, less: (a: *void, b: *void) -> bool) {
    if count <= 1 || element_size <= 0 { return; }
    if count <= SORT_INSERTION_MAX {
        sort_by_insertion(data, count, element_size, less);
        return;
    }
    bytes := cast(*u8) data;
    spare := cast(*u8) scratch;
    half := count / 2;
    right := bytes + half * element_size;
    sort_by_merging(bytes, scratch, half, element_size, less);
    sort_by_merging(right, scratch, count - half, element_size, less);
    if !less(right, right - element_size) { return; }

    copy_memory(spare, bytes, half * element_size);
    i := 0;
    j := half;
    k := 0;
    while i < half && j < count {
        from := spare + i * element_size;
        if less(bytes + j * element_size, from) {
            from = bytes + j * element_size;
            j += 1;
        } else {
            i += 1;
        }
        copy_memory(bytes + k * element_size, from, element_size);
        k += 1;
    }
    copy_memory(bytes + k * element_size, spare + i * element_size, (half - i) * element_size);
}

// Sorts the count elements of element_size bytes each that start at data,
// in place, so that no element is less than one before it, by less, which
// is given pointers to two elements and says whether the first goes before
// the second. Equal elements, neither less than the other, keep the order
// they had: the sort is stable. less must be a strict weak order, as C++'s
// sorts ask: nothing is less than itself, a less than b and b less than c
// means a is less than c, and two elements equal to a third are equal to
// each other. One that isn't leaves the elements in some order, each still
// there once, but not necessarily a sorted one, and equal elements may lose
// their order. Either way nothing outside the elements is read or written,
// since no loop's bounds depend on what less says.
//
// The scratch memory, half the elements' bytes, comes from allocator, the
// context's unless given, and goes back to it before sort returns. When
// the allocator has none, or the bytes would be more than an int can
// count, the elements are sorted by insertion in place instead, still
// stable. Up to SORT_INSERTION_MAX elements are sorted by insertion
// without asking for memory. Nothing is asked for, compared or touched for
// a count of 1 or less, or an element_size of 0 or less, so data may be
// null then.
sort :: (data: *void, count: int, element_size: int, less: (a: *void, b: *void) -> bool, allocator: Allocator = context.allocator) {
    LARGEST :: 9223372036854775807; // int's largest value
    if count <= 1 || element_size <= 0 { return; }
    if count <= SORT_INSERTION_MAX {
        sort_by_insertion(data, count, element_size, less);
        return;
    }
    half := count / 2;
    scratch : *void = null;
    if half <= LARGEST / element_size { scratch = alloc(half * element_size, allocator); }
    if scratch == null {
        sort_by_insertion(data, count, element_size, less);
        return;
    }
    sort_by_merging(data, scratch, count, element_size, less);
    free(scratch, allocator);
}

// The first index of the count sorted elements of element_size bytes each
// that start at data whose element isn't below key, and whether the element
// there is equal to it: C++'s lower_bound. So for a key that is there, the
// index is its first occurrence's; for one that isn't, it is where the key
// would go to keep the elements sorted, and the result is that index and
// false; past the last element, it is count and false.
//
// compare is given key first and an element second, and gives a negative
// number when the key goes before the element, 0 when they're equal, and
// a positive number when it goes after: compare_int and compare_string
// give -1, 0 or 1. The key needn't be an element: a procedure can compare
// an int with a struct's int field, to find a struct by it. The elements
// must be sorted by the same order. Each step halves the elements still to
// look at, so it takes about log2(count) + 1 comparisons.
//
// A count of 0 or less, or an element_size of 0 or less, has no element to
// find: 0 and false, without calling compare, so data may be null then.
binary_search :: (data: *void, count: int, element_size: int, key: *void, compare: (key: *void, element: *void) -> int) -> int, bool {
    if count <= 0 || element_size <= 0 { return 0, false; }
    bytes := cast(*u8) data;
    // The answer is always from low to high, both included: every element
    // before low is below the key, and high is either count or an element
    // that isn't. The middle is worked out as low + (high - low) / 2 rather
    // than (low + high) / 2, which could overflow for the largest counts.
    low := 0;
    high := count;
    while low < high {
        middle := low + (high - low) / 2;
        if compare(key, bytes + middle * element_size) > 0 {
            low = middle + 1;
        } else {
            high = middle;
        }
    }
    if low < count && compare(key, bytes + low * element_size) == 0 { return low, true; }
    return low, false;
}

// Whether the int at a is less than the int at b, for sort.
less_int :: (a: *void, b: *void) -> bool {
    return (cast(*int) a).* < (cast(*int) b).*;
}

// Whether the float64 at a is less than the float64 at b, for sort, with
// NaN after every number. Every comparison with NaN is false, so x < y
// alone would make a NaN equal to every number, and 1 equal to NaN equal
// to 2 while 1 is less than 2: no strict weak order, and the sort could
// leave the NaNs' neighbours unsorted. So when b is NaN, a goes first
// unless it is NaN too (a NaN is the one value not equal to itself); two
// NaNs are equal. -0.0 and 0.0 are equal, and keep their order.
less_float64 :: (a: *void, b: *void) -> bool {
    x := (cast(*float64) a).*;
    y := (cast(*float64) b).*;
    if y != y { return x == x; }
    return x < y;
}

// Whether the string at a sorts before the string at b, for sort: by
// compare_strings, byte by byte, unsigned, a prefix first.
less_string :: (a: *void, b: *void) -> bool {
    return compare_strings((cast(*string) a).*, (cast(*string) b).*) < 0;
}

// -1, 0 or 1 as the int at key is less than, equal to or greater than the
// int at element, for binary_search.
compare_int :: (key: *void, element: *void) -> int {
    k := (cast(*int) key).*;
    e := (cast(*int) element).*;
    if k < e { return -1; }
    if k > e { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

// compare_strings of the string at key and the string at element, for
// binary_search: it takes pointers to the two strings, where
// compare_strings takes the strings.
compare_string :: (key: *void, element: *void) -> int {
    return compare_strings((cast(*string) key).*, (cast(*string) element).*);
}
